<description>
Recolor the grey object with the color of the marker set closest to it, erasing all markers.

Input structure
- The grid has a black background with one 4-connected grey object.
- Every other foreground color appears as one or more isolated marker pixels.

Rule
- For each marker color, measure the smallest squared Euclidean distance from any marker pixel of that color to any grey-object pixel.
- The unique marker color with the smallest such distance becomes the output color.
- Return a blank background grid with the grey object's mask painted in that selected color.

Key insight
- The scattered pixels are competing color labels; only the label with the closest approach to the grey object survives.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_to_match",
  "src": "candidate marker color sets s, each made of isolated non-grey foreground pixels",
  "dst": "the single grey object d",
  "match_cond": "s has the unique smallest squared Euclidean distance from any marker pixel in s to any pixel of d",
  "transform": "erase all markers and recolor d with s.color on the black background"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 10x10, black background
  grey_object:
    shape: single 4-connected grey (5) object, LR- and UD-symmetric [observed]
    pixels: 4-13
    position: centered near grid middle (centroid within 1 cell of center) [observed]
    examples: horizontal bar, vertical bar, rectangle, diamond, plus-shaped cross
  markers:
    color_count: 2-3 distinct non-grey non-black colors
    per_color_pixels: 1-6 isolated single pixels (no two same-color pixels 4-adjacent)
    total_pixels: 2-14
    placement: scattered across grid, never on or 4-adjacent to grey object
  distance_rule:
    metric: minimum squared Euclidean distance from any marker pixel of that color to any grey pixel
    winner: unique color with smallest distance; gap to second-best >= 2
  output: black grid with grey object repainted in winning color; all markers erased

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick 2-3 marker colors + grey; all distinct, non-black.
    2. Place a symmetric grey object (4-13 pixels) near grid center.
    3. Designate one color as winner. Place 1-6 winner-color marker pixels
       with min squared distance D_win to grey (D_win >= 1).
    4. For each remaining color, place 1-6 marker pixels ensuring their
       min squared distance to grey > D_win (gap >= 2). Reject if tie.
    5. Verify all markers are isolated (no same-color 4-adjacency) and
       not on/adjacent-to grey. The output is the grey mask repainted with winner color.
```
